eading across then down: the curve reaches cf = 20 at a walking time of about 25 minutes (the points on the curve are (0, 0), (10, 5), (20, 15), (30, 25), (40, 35), (50, 40); cf 15 at 20 min and cf 25 at 30 min give cf 20 at 25 min). DERIVED from the printed curve.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Cumulative Frequency Curves","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":28,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Use cf = n ÷ 2 = 20, read across and down.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["35 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes","25 minutes"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Halve 40 to get the median position on the y-axis.","Cf = 20 lies halfway between the plotted points (20 min, 15) and (30 min, 25)."],"figure":"myp4_q28_ogive"},"marking":""},{"n":29,"t":"single_choice","p":"The table shows the marks out of 50 obtained by 50 students in a history test. Which set of points gives the correct cumulative frequency curve for these marks?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The table shows the marks out of 50 obtained by 50 students in a history test. Which set of points gives the correct cumulative frequency curve for these marks?"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mark m"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequency f"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 ≤ m < 10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10 ≤ m < 20"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"11"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20 ≤ m < 30"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"14"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30 ≤ m < 40"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"13"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40 ≤ m < 50"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Total number of students"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"50"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Cumulative frequencies: 5, 5 + 11 = 16, 16 + 14 = 30, 30 + 13 = 43, 43 + 7 = 50. An ogive plots upper class boundaries against cumulative frequency, starting at (0, 0): (0, 0), (10, 5), (20, 16), (30, 30), (40, 43), (50, 50). The midpoints option misplaces the x-coordinates; option D plots frequencies, not running totals; option B omits the origin.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Cumulative Frequency Curves","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":29,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Plot upper class boundaries against running totals, starting at (0, 0).","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["(5, 5), (15, 16), (25, 30), (35, 43), (45, 50)","(10, 5), (20, 16), (30, 30), (40, 43), (50, 50)","(0, 0), (10, 5), (20, 16), (30, 30), (40, 43), (50, 50)","(0, 0), (10, 5), (20, 11), (30, 14), (40, 13), (50, 7)"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Add the frequencies one class at a time, keeping a running total.","Ogives use upper class boundaries and include the origin (0, 0)."]},"marking":""},{"n":30,"t":"single_choice","p":"The curve shows the masses, in kilograms, of tomatoes harvested from each of the 32 plots in a market garden near Nsawkw. Estimate the values of Q1, Q2 and Q3 from the curve.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The curve shows the masses, in kilograms, of tomatoes harvested from each of the 32 plots in a market garden near Nsawkw. Estimate the values of Q1, Q2 and Q3 from the curve."}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q30_ogive.jpg","alt":"Ogive of tomato yields from 32 plots","width":96}}]},"e":"n = 32: Q1 position = 8, Q2 (median) = 16, Q3 = 24. Reading the curve at cf 8 → mass ≈ 30 kg; cf 16 → ≈ 35 kg; cf 24 → ≈ 40 kg. (The plotted points are (20, 0), (25, 2), (30, 8), (35, 16), (40, 24), (45, 30), (50, 32).) Values 8, 16, 24 are frequencies, not masses.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Cumulative Frequency Curves","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":30,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Positions 8, 16 and 24 on the y-axis — then read the masses.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["25, 30 and 35 kg","20, 35 and 50 kg","8, 16 and 24 kg","30, 35 and 40 kg"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Q1, Q2, Q3 sit at n÷4, n÷2, 3n÷4 = 8, 16, 24 on the y-axis.","Read across at cf = 8, 16, 24 and down to the mass axis."],"figure":"myp4_q30_ogive"},"marking":""},{"n":31,"t":"single_choice","p":"A school in Bo gives a certificate to every student who scores at least 40 marks on the test in Question 26, whose curve is shown here. What percentage of the 60 students received a certificate?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A school in Bo gives a certificate to every student who scores at least 40 marks on the test in Question 26, whose curve is shown here. What percentage of the 60 students received a certificate?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q31_ogive.jpg","alt":"Ogive of the marks of 60 students (same curve as Question 26)","width":96}}]},"e":"From the curve, cf at mark 40 is 45 — that is the number of students who scored less than 40. At least 40 marks: 60 − 45 = 15 students. Percentage = 15 ÷ 60 × 100 = 25%.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Cumulative Frequency Curves","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":31,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Students with at least 40 marks = total − cf at 40.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["75%","45%","25%","15%"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Read cf at mark 40 from the curve (it is 45).","Subtract the reading from the total, then make it a percentage of 60."],"figure":"myp4_q31_ogive"},"marking":""},{"n":32,"t":"single_choice","p":"The histogram shows the masses, in kilograms, of 50 bags of rice in a warehouse in Lomé. How many bags have a mass between 25 kg and 30 kg?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The histogram shows the masses, in kilograms, of 50 bags of rice in a warehouse in Lomé. How many bags have a mass between 25 kg and 30 kg?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q32_rice.jpg","alt":"Histogram of masses of 50 bags of rice","width":96}}]},"e":"The bar for 25 ≤ m < 30 reaches a height of 14 on the frequency axis, so 14 bags have a mass between 25 and 30 kg. (Check the total: 6 + 14 + 12 + 10 + 8 = 50 ✓.)\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":32,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Read the height of the 25–30 kg bar.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["6 bags","14 bags","20 bags","12 bags"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Find the bar spanning 25 to 30 on the mass axis.","Its height on the frequency axis is the answer."],"figure":"myp4_q32_rice"},"marking":""},{"n":33,"t":"single_choice","p":"The same 50 bags of rice are drawn as a relative-frequency histogram. What is the height of the bar for the class 25 ≤ m < 30, to 2 decimal places?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The same 50 bags of rice are drawn as a relative-frequency histogram. What is the height of the bar for the class 25 ≤ m < 30, to 2 decimal places?"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tab